WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.379
You’re a pizza delivery person for a company
we’re not at liberty to name.

00:00:04.379 --> 00:00:09.170
It may seem like a thankless job, but you’re
part of the thin crust between satisfaction

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:13.360
and starvation for people who really don’t
feel like cooking or going out tonight.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.849
You’re a tried and true pizza veteran.

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:20.180
You’ve seen a lot on the job - from people
who tried to rob you on their own doorsteps,

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:25.880
to one guy who tried to tip you with a clearly
fake Rolex - but you haven’t seen anything

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.310
like the insanity you’re about to encounter
tonight.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.949
That’s because you’re about to get sick.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:34.150
Really, really sick.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:39.781
And we know there’s probably a lot of diseases
you’d rather not catch, but trust us, no

00:00:39.781 --> 00:00:44.410
disease bites quite like SCP-3760.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:45.510
But back to you.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:49.730
You’re parked on the saddle of your prized
bicycle, pedaling for your life, the biggest

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:55.030
order you’ve ever delivered strapped to
the cargo rack behind you - eight large meat-feast

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:56.030
pizzas.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.520
You figure you’re heading towards the most
hopping, or at least the hungriest party in

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.580
the city, and despite the frustration of needing
to ferry this hot, cheesy nightmare to its

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.570
destination, parties have historically been
pretty good tippers.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:13.550
Little do you know, your prized customer is
about to give you much more than just a tip.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:16.510
You pull up outside their ground level apartment
and park your bike.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:20.220
There’s no music coming from the apartment
you’re heading to.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.220
Strange.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:25.000
You pick up your precious cargo and trudge
towards the door, barely managing to ring

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.200
the bell with your spare pinky.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.890
The door swings open instantly, as though
the occupant was just standing behind it the

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.140
whole time, waiting for you.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.690
When the door opens, you’re not sure what
to make of what you see inside.

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:39.690
There isn’t a party.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.170
There doesn’t even seem to be a group.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:48.690
Just one person, standing there and staring
at you, and something about this person is

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.130
just...wrong.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.970
His skin seems to be an oddly unnatural color.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:58.280
It has a slightly gray hue, like it’s rotting,
and you see that the stranger’s shirt is

00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.990
torn in several places.

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:04.130
You’re trying your best to remain professional
and not stare, but you can’t help but notice

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:07.420
that whatever is under these tears doesn’t
look like skin.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:13.640
It’s almost like there are holes all over
his body underneath the shirt - big, gaping

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:14.980
holes.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:15.980
And are those…

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:19.590
You’re cut off when he takes the pile of
pizzas from you, and shoves what was once

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.610
a handful of dollars into your palm.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:25.310
They seem torn up, and worst of all, wet.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:29.290
You look past your strange customer, and see
that his apartment looks just as shoddy as

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:30.290
he does.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:32.430
There’s stuffing torn from the couch.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:33.980
Huge tears in the curtains.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:38.540
What you could swear are scratch or bite marks
on some of the wooden furniture.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.010
This man clearly needed his pizza.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:46.319
As you begin to step away, happy to just be
rid of this freaky situation, you do something

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:48.580
that you’ll live to regret.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.770
Just for a moment, you lock eyes with the
customer.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.820
There’s something about them that makes
you feel sick.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:03:00.360
They’re wet and black - more like a doll’s
eyes than anything that should belong to a

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:01.360
human.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.660
Just that one instant of contact is enough
to chill your bones, and according to some

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:11.280
theories around SCP-3760, it may have been
the moment of your infection.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.560
As the stranger closes the door in your face,
presumably to eat his obscene number of pizzas,

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.960
you stumble back to your bicycle, letting
your torn up, gooey tip blow away in the wind.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.180
Getting out of this place is rewarding enough.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.690
But you can’t chase the thoughts of the
stranger out of your mind.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:28.760
Was he sick?

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.680
Was he even human?

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.099
These are the thoughts swimming through your
head as you cycle back to the restaurant.

00:03:34.099 --> 00:03:38.750
You’re so preoccupied, you don’t even
notice the truck speeding towards you.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:42.060
You get sideswiped, destroying your bike and
throwing you through the air.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:45.150
You can feel the snap of your arm breaking
as you land.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:49.190
It’s a horrible feeling to go along with
the horrible noise.

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:53.950
And as you fade from consciousness, the last
thought in your mind is the black, soulless

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.769
eyes of the customer.

00:03:55.769 --> 00:03:59.660
When you wake up, you find that you’re in
a hospital bed with your arm in a cast.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:03.849
The doctors tell you that you’re phenomenally
lucky to be alive given the severity of the

00:04:03.849 --> 00:04:04.849
crash.

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:08.490
The doctor says that you’re dealing with
a compound fracture in your right arm, as

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:13.080
well as some nasty cuts that required stitches,
but otherwise you’re fine.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:18.069
The hospital insists that you at least stay
the night though, and you oblige, but that

00:04:18.069 --> 00:04:21.329
night you find it’s difficult to sleep.

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:26.559
Partly because of the pain, but mostly because
of an extremely strange sound coming from

00:04:26.559 --> 00:04:28.689
inside the cast around your arm.

00:04:28.689 --> 00:04:33.659
It’s a scraping noise, like something sharp
scratching at the interior.

00:04:33.659 --> 00:04:37.919
You figure it must just be some strange side
effect of the pain meds - you’re not going

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:40.789
to let worry eat you up inside.

00:04:40.789 --> 00:04:43.830
Something else will be doing that soon enough.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:47.629
You’re discharged from the hospital the
next day and told that you should be fine

00:04:47.629 --> 00:04:51.460
in a couple months, as though that’s some
kind of consolation.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:56.279
You try to carry on with your daily life,
as much as you can with one arm out of commission,

00:04:56.279 --> 00:05:00.309
but that awful crunching noise from inside
your cast persists.

00:05:00.309 --> 00:05:05.789
You even start to become paranoid that some
sort of insect is trapped inside, or worse,

00:05:05.789 --> 00:05:07.569
a swarm of insects.

00:05:07.569 --> 00:05:11.110
But you aren’t left with that uncertainty
for long.

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:13.500
By the next morning, your cast has fallen
off.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.900
Well, not “fallen”, per se.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.979
A more accurate description would be that
it was chewed off from the inside.

00:05:19.979 --> 00:05:25.409
And not by something trapped in there with
your arm, but by your arm itself.

00:05:25.409 --> 00:05:29.650
When you finally see it, it doesn’t even
look like an arm anymore: The flesh has turned

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:30.650
gray.

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:35.090
That same sickly gray of the customer’s
skin, the night of your accident.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:40.039
But worse still: From the top of the hand
down to the base of the forearm, your entire

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:43.969
arm is split open into a fang-lined, chattering
mouth.

00:05:43.969 --> 00:05:47.879
When you’ve gotten over the initial horror
of seeing the mutations that have taken hold

00:05:47.879 --> 00:05:52.080
of your arm, you notice something about the
teeth in the second mouth: They’re nothing

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.779
like human teeth, they’re vicious, jagged
triangles, each one like a dagger.

00:05:57.779 --> 00:06:00.960
These are unmistakably shark’s teeth.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.889
You give yourself the luxury of having a good
scream - After all, what else are you supposed

00:06:04.889 --> 00:06:09.759
to do after you find your arm has turned into
some kind of fleshy shark mutant.

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:13.980
You notice that you can hardly even feel the
snapping of its jaws, like it isn’t even

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:15.550
really a part of you.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:19.949
And as much as you try to tense and release
what used to be the tendons in your forearm,

00:06:19.949 --> 00:06:21.759
there is no change.

00:06:21.759 --> 00:06:27.800
For all intents and purposes, that limb is
no longer yours, it just happens to be attached

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:28.800
to you.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.979
Not only that, but it’s always very, very
hungry, as quickly became evident from the

00:06:33.979 --> 00:06:39.190
fact it made such short work of the cast that’d
been containing it not long ago.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:43.839
You can see that the graying flesh is beginning
to spread from your forearm to your bicep.

00:06:43.839 --> 00:06:47.960
It’s almost like, little by little, your
tissue is being switched.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:54.050
Somehow, you’re innately aware that if you
don’t feed your arm, it’ll get even worse.

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:58.439
Suddenly, everything about the strange customer
from the night of the accident makes sense:

00:06:58.439 --> 00:07:01.539
He had whatever you have now, only worse.

00:07:01.539 --> 00:07:06.169
He was just hoping that the pizzas would keep
the beast at bay, and you very much hope the

00:07:06.169 --> 00:07:08.789
contents of your fridge will do the same.

00:07:08.789 --> 00:07:13.759
Wasting no time, you grab some processed ham
and raw chicken - you’re not an idiot, you

00:07:13.759 --> 00:07:17.619
know that if this thing is anything like a
real shark, it’s only going to be interested

00:07:17.619 --> 00:07:18.669
in meat.

00:07:18.669 --> 00:07:22.490
But when you shove the processed meat into
the nibbling maw in your forearm, you notice

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:24.990
that it seems to spit the food out.

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:28.289
Something tells you that your offering was
too… dead.

00:07:28.289 --> 00:07:30.360
Now, you’re really afraid.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.679
Will this thing want live prey?

00:07:32.679 --> 00:07:37.159
Will that be the only way to stop it from
taking the rest of your arm, or even more?

00:07:37.159 --> 00:07:38.539
The thought terrifies you.

00:07:38.539 --> 00:07:43.729
It’s like being Venom, except you don’t
even get a new alien friend or any cool superpowers

00:07:43.729 --> 00:07:44.939
for your trouble.

00:07:44.939 --> 00:07:47.189
But you’re smart; you’re resourceful.

00:07:47.189 --> 00:07:50.870
You don’t intend to let this thing take
over your life without a fight, and you’ve

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:55.099
always believed what GI Joe taught you: Knowing
is half the battle.

00:07:55.099 --> 00:07:59.479
Sadly, doing an internet search for “My
injury is somehow mutating into a shark, how

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:01.990
do I stop this” didn’t do much good.

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:05.710
And you don't want to go to a doctor, because
part of you expects to be immediately shipped

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:09.039
off to the nearest circus or military research
lab.

00:08:09.039 --> 00:08:13.000
Instead, you throw on a thick leather jacket
that you hope your new shark-arm will take

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:17.789
a while to eat through, and head to the library
of the nearest medical college.

00:08:17.789 --> 00:08:23.469
If anywhere has sources on the most obscure
of medical problems, this would be the place.

00:08:23.469 --> 00:08:28.360
And after hours of searching through index
cards, you find it - what seems to be the

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:33.780
one and only source on this ludicrous topic
in the thousands and thousands of books: A

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:38.460
series of journal entries made by Dr. Keith
Woodward, Ship's Medical Officer aboard the

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:41.620
USS Kirby, circa 1922.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:46.230
Even the library’s indexing system prefaces
the source with a disclaimer that it’s likely

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:52.060
25% quackery and 75% ocean madness, but that
doesn’t deter you.

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:53.740
Any port in a storm.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:58.520
Dr. Woodward told the horrifying tale of the
USS Kirby’s collapse due to a sudden and

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:00.530
unexplainable contagion.

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:04.500
It began with the infection of a sailor on
the ship named Willis Riggs, who’d fallen

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:07.980
overboard and had his foot partially eaten
by a shark.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:12.610
Much like your arm, the man’s foot slowly
became the ravenous, all-devouring mouth of

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:14.360
a shark itself.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:18.320
Little by little, it spread to other crew
members through unknown means.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:22.520
Any injuries sustained by the crew didn’t
heal like normal, but instead became this

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:24.560
vicious, living shark tissue.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:30.560
Soon, these ever-hungry new mouths led them
to eat each other, and then themselves, from

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:31.780
inside out.

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:36.330
Even after the humans they once were could
be considered long dead, those hungry jaws

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:37.910
kept snapping.

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:39.500
There was no cure.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:41.720
There was no solution.

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:45.920
Everyone on the boat was recorded as having
mysteriously died at sea.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.070
That is the curse of SCP-3760.

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:54.530
As if on cue, you look down to see that the
shark mouth in your arm has chewed through

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:57.300
the leather of your sleeve like it’s nothing.

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:00.370
It’s hungry, and nothing will satiate it.

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:05.440
But perhaps some sacrifices need to be made
if you want to stop it from getting worse…

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:10.390
Just as the thought crosses your mind, a needle
full of sedative is injected into your neck.

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:14.340
Everything goes dark very quickly, and when
you wake up, you’re in a clean, clinical

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:15.390
white room.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:20.101
You’re now officially under containment
by the SCP Foundation, not that you’ll know

00:10:20.101 --> 00:10:21.860
that any time soon.

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:27.340
This may seem like the cavalry has arrived,
but sadly for your sake, they have no interest

00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:28.610
in curing you.

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:33.470
They’re just eager to gather more data on
how your condition progresses.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:39.660
SCP-3760 exists as a latent infection in well
over half of humanity, but its horrific, mutating

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:42.580
effects are activated in only a relative few.

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:50.490
There have only been 2,785 recorded cases
of SCP-3760 activity observed between 1958

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:52.600
and 2016.

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.330
This is thanks to the Foundation’s secret
weapon against the shark mutation menace - Subliminal

00:10:57.330 --> 00:11:03.040
Memetic Agent Kappa/Omicron - or "SMA-K/O"
- a widely distributed memetic agent that

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:06.540
suppresses the symptoms of infection in most
people.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:11.190
Not that this will provide much comfort to
you, as you join the almost 3,000 others who

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:17.110
have been infected and you are doomed to slowly
turn into an abomination in your containment

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:18.110
chamber.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:22.710
If it’s any consolation, you may not see
the truly advanced stages of the disease.

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:27.060
The most common cause of death among the afflicted
is being eaten by their own ravenous shark

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:30.360
mouths long before it changes your entire
body.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:34.100
Actually, come to think of it, that’s not
much of a consolation at all.

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:38.670
But hey, when it comes to anomalous mutations,
we’re sad to report that there’s rarely

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:45.800
a happy ending in store for those afflicted,
and SCP-3760 is no exception to the rule.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:51.481
Now check out “SCP-4205 - In The Eyes of
the Beholder” and “SCP-610 - The Flesh

00:11:51.481 --> 00:11:55.740
That Hates” for more anomalous infections
you really don’t want to catch!

